  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/R-O E-427 vsFoodInspector on 2 February, 2017.json",
  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "R/O E-427 vs Food Inspector on 2 February, 2017 1 In The Court Of Sh. Rakesh Pandit, Asj-01, New Delhi District, Patiala House Courts, New Delhi Ca No. 8661/16 Rakesh Goyal @ Joginder Goyal S/o Sh. T. R. Goyal M/s Goel Store, E-418, New Ashok Nagar, Delhi-110096. R/o E-427, New Ashok Nagar, Delhi-110096. ....Appellant Vs. Food Inspector Department of Pfa Govt. Of Nct of Delhi A-20, Lawrence Road Industrial Area, Delhi-110035 ..... Respondent Date of receiving of Appeal : 04.06.2016 Date of arguments : 02.02.2017 Date of judgment : 03.02.2017 Judgment",
    "facts": "2 The brief facts of the case as per record and from trial court record are that accused is running an establishment namely M/s Goel Store, E-418, New Ashok Nagar, Delhi-110096. On 23.03.2009 at about 7.30 p.m. Food Inspector Sh. V. P. S. Chaudhary visited the said place and purchased 1500 grams of \"Dal Moth\" (ready for sale) from accused. FI was accompanied with SDM/LHA Sh. Radha Charan. The said Moth Dal was purchased for the purposes of analysis under PFA Act. The same sample was divided into three parts, packed as per Rules of PFA. Panchanama was prepared. One sample was sent for analysis to Public Analyst. As per the report of Analyst, sample did not confirm to the standard because \"The sample is adulterated because it is coloured with synthetic colouring matter viz. Tartrazine.\" R/O E-427 vs Food Inspector on 2 February, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/57493807/ 1 3 As the sample was failed, prosecution was launched by Food Inspector by filing complaint dated 23.11.2009 with the court. Accused was summoned. He has exercised his right u/sec.13(2) PFA Act to get the sample analyzed by CFL. CFL reported \"I am of the opinion that the sample does not conform to the standards of \"Any other food grains\" as per PFA Rules 1955 (Synthetic colour Tartrazine detected).\" Rakesh Goyal @ Joginder Goyal Vs. State 4 Charge was framed against accused u/sec.2 (a) (j) & (m) read with sec.7 of PFA Act, 1954 punishable u/sec.16(1A) of PFA Act. 5 6 In this appeal, the accused has assailed the judgment on the following grounds:- (i) There is variation in PA and CFL Report. (ii) Non-compliance of Rule 14 of PFA Rules i.e. the bottles, jhawa, polythene were not made clean and dried at the spot by the food inspector or any other official. (iii) Chromatography test is not a reliable test. 7 No reply was filed by respondent/state and the matter was argued orally. This line of argument was done before the trial court and was dealt in para 13 to 43 of the judgment.",
    "arguments": "I do not find any other opinion than that given by the trial court and thus the arguments addressed by Ld. counsel for appellant in this regard are rejected and the reasoning given by the R/O E-427 vs Food Inspector on 2 February, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/57493807/ 2 trial court is upheld. However, this fact was elaborated further by Hon. Supreme Court in Varghese Vs. Food, 1989 (II) Prevention of Food Adulteration Cases, 236 stating that \"it must be the endeavour of the FI to use clean and dry implements in sampling the articles of food. If unhygienic methods are adopted, it will effect the result of analysis. So, the Hon. Supreme Court use the word \"implements\" instead of only \"dry and clean bottles/containers\". Similarly, in other judgments, i.e. State of Haryana Vs. Kishan Kumar CLT 559, it was observed that the prosecution had not stated that the \"weighing scale\" was clean. Similarly, in other judgments, like Sardar Mal Jain, 1996 (2) Prevention of Food Adulteration Cases, 203, it was stated that the newspaper on which the Barfi was weighed was not clean. Similarly, in Shashi Kant Vs. State of UP, 1983 (1) Prevention of Food Adulteration Cases 90, it was observed that the \"bhagona\" was not proved to be clean and dry. Similarly, in 2008 (3) RCR (Criminal) 422 Rajinder Kumar Vs. State of Haryana (Punjab and Haryana), it was held by the court that when an envelop is used for weighing the s a m p l e c o m m o d i t y , t h e s a m e m u s t b e p r o v e d a s Rakesh Goyal @ Joginder Goyal Vs. State clean and free from any impurity. 14 So, accordingly, it can be said that the law which can be crystallized from various judgments of Hon. High Courts as well as Hon. Supreme Court is that if in evidence, the prosecution states that if the sample commodity had touched a surface of an \"implement\", it is the duty of the prosecution to prove beyond reasonable doubt that the said implement was clean and dry and thus could not have added any impurity to the sample.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "I do not find any other opinion than that given by the trial court and thus the arguments addressed by Ld. counsel for appellant in this regard are rejected and the reasoning given by the R/O E-427 vs Food Inspector on 2 February, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/57493807/ 2 trial court is upheld.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "However, this fact was elaborated further by Hon. Supreme Court in Varghese Vs. Food, 1989 (II) Prevention of Food Adulteration Cases, 236 stating that \"it must be the endeavour of the FI to use clean and dry implements in sampling the articles of food. If unhygienic methods are adopted, it will effect the result of analysis. So, the Hon. Supreme Court use the word \"implements\" instead of only \"dry and clean bottles/containers\". Similarly, in other judgments, i.e. State of Haryana Vs. Kishan Kumar CLT 559, it was observed that the prosecution had not stated that the \"weighing scale\" was clean. Similarly, in other judgments, like Sardar Mal Jain, 1996 (2) Prevention of Food Adulteration Cases, 203, it was stated that the newspaper on which the Barfi was weighed was not clean. Similarly, in Shashi Kant Vs. State of UP, 1983 (1) Prevention of Food Adulteration Cases 90, it was observed that the \"bhagona\" was not proved to be clean and dry. Similarly, in 2008 (3) RCR (Criminal) 422 Rajinder Kumar Vs. State of Haryana (Punjab and Haryana), it was held by the court that when an envelop is used for weighing the s a m p l e c o m m o d i t y , t h e s a m e m u s t b e p r o v e d a s Rakesh Goyal @ Joginder Goyal Vs. State clean and free from any impurity. 14 So, accordingly, it can be said that the law which can be crystallized from various judgments of Hon. High Courts as well as Hon. Supreme Court is that if in evidence, the prosecution states that if the sample commodity had touched a surface of an \"implement\", it is the duty of the prosecution to prove beyond reasonable doubt that the said implement was clean and dry and thus could not have added any impurity to the sample."
  },
